<div class="book-title">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim</div>
<div class="halacha-title">Hilchos Betzi'as HaPas — Halachah 5<br><span style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:400;">Part 2: §§ 14–26</span></div>
<div class="lm-subtitle">Continuation of <span class="v">"L'Hamshich Shalom"</span> <span class="sr">(<span class="lr">LM I, 14</span>)</span></div>

<!-- §14 -->
<div class="sn">§ 14 — The Oil of <i>Chanukah</i>: Holy <i>Da'as</i> of Torah</div>

<div class="p">The oil of the <span class="h">Chanukah</span> light = the holy <span class="h">da'as</span> [knowledge/awareness] of Torah: <span class="h">shemen mish'chas kodesh</span> [holy anointing oil]. Through the <span class="h">mitzvah</span> of lighting <span class="h">ner Chanukah</span>, we draw upon ourselves the holiness of the <span class="h">da'as</span> of Torah that flows through the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span>. Through this we are purified from the soiled garments and merit white garments: <span class="v">"at all times let your garments be white, and let oil upon your head never be lacking."</span> <span class="kc">"Oil upon your head never be lacking"</span> = the oil of <span class="h">ner Chanukah</span> = engagement in Torah that must never cease. Through this: <span class="v">"at all times let your garments be white"</span> — <span class="kc">at all times</span> precisely! Even though the soiled garments try to reassert themselves constantly, by fleeing to Torah each time, one purifies one's garments through the Torah's waters and merits white garments at all times.</div>

<div class="p">This is the meaning of the verse praising Torah-study: <span class="v">"therefore you were anointed with oil of joy above your fellows; myrrh and aloes and cassia — all your garments…"</span> Through "oil of joy" = "oil upon your head" — the garments emit a beautiful fragrance = white garments, the opposite of soiled garments whose stench is foul. The Sages: <span class="mx">"all your betrayals [<span class="h">begidosecha</span>] emit a good fragrance"</span> — Torah purifies all blemishes clinging to the garments until sins are transformed into merits, and the betrayals (<span class="h">begidos</span> = garment-blemishes) themselves emit a good fragrance.</div>

<!-- §15 -->
<div class="sn">§ 15 — <i>Mosif V'Holech</i>: Adding Light Each Day</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Ner Chanukah</span> is <span class="h">mosif v'holech</span> — one adds a candle each day. Through the great miracle of <span class="h">Chanukah</span>, we merit to increase and expand the light of Torah daily until the <span class="kc">eighth day</span> — the essence of the miracle, when the small amount of oil burned for eight days. The eighth day = <span class="h">Binah</span>, <span class="h">Imma Ila'ah</span> [the Supernal Mother] = the root of Torah, which encompasses the seven days / seven <span class="h">middos</span> / seven branches of the <span class="h">Menorah</span>. Their root is in <span class="h">Binah</span> = the eighth day, where the root-souls of Israel dwell.</div>

<div class="p">This is why <span class="h">Parshas Va'Atah Tetzaveh</span> — speaking of the <span class="h">Menorah</span>'s oil — is immediately followed by <span class="h">Parshas Bigdei Kehunah</span> [the priestly garments]. Through the <span class="h">Menorah</span>'s oil = <span class="h">ner Chanukah</span>, one merits white garments = priestly garments. The miracle of <span class="h">Chanukah</span> was through the <span class="h">Kohanim</span>, who wear priestly garments = white garments.</div>

<!-- §16 -->
<div class="sn">§ 16 — The <i>Bais HaMikdash</i>: All <i>Tikkunim</i> in One Place</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Chanukah</span> = inauguration of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>, because all the <span class="h">tikkunim</span> above are contained there. The <span class="h">Luchos</span> [Tablets] and <span class="h">Sefer Torah</span> rested in the <span class="h">Kodesh HaKodashim</span> upon the <span class="h">Even HaSh'siyah</span> [Foundation Stone]. The <span class="h">Sanhedrin</span> sat there: <span class="v">"from Tziyon shall Torah go forth."</span> There, purification from all sins (= soiled garments) is accomplished through the Torah emanating from there: <span class="mx">"a spring from the House of Hashem goes forth, ever strengthening."</span></div>

<div class="p">There = the essential rise of <span class="h">kavod</span>: <span class="v">"the glory of Hashem filled the House."</span> There = the essential drawing-near of converts and <span class="h">ba'alei teshuvah</span>: <span class="v">"peoples shall call to the mountain"</span> (Rashi: referring to converts); <span class="v">"My House shall be called a house of prayer for all peoples."</span> There = the essential <span class="h">yirah</span>: <span class="v">"how awesome is this place!"</span> Avraham called it <span class="v">"Hashem Yireh"</span> [Hashem will see/be seen]. There = the essential <span class="h">shalom</span>: Malki Tzedek named it <span class="h">Shalem</span>. Hashem combined both names: <span class="kc">Yerushalayim = <span class="h">yirah</span> + <span class="h">shalom</span></span>. The essential peace is through <span class="h">yirah</span> completed through <span class="h">kavod</span> — all accomplished in the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> standing in Yerushalayim.</div>

<!-- §17 -->
<div class="sn">§ 17 — <i>Batei Midrash</i>: The <i>Mikdash Me'at</i></div>

<div class="p">The holiness of <span class="h">batei knesses</span> and <span class="h">batei midrash</span> = <span class="h">mikdash me'at</span> [miniature sanctuaries]. The <span class="h">tikkun</span> is through Torah-study of the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who can arouse the root-souls in Torah. Every person must draw this holiness upon himself by <span class="kc">connecting to the <span class="h">yirei Hashem</span> who are attached to the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span></span>: <span class="v">"I am a companion to all who fear You."</span> A lone individual finds it hard to draw this illumination alone, but when many join together and build a <span class="h">bais midrash</span> for Torah — whose intention is toward truth, to be included through Torah and prayer in the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who have the power to illuminate all souls — then surely <span class="v">"G-d the mighty does not despise"</span>, for the merit of the many is greater.</div>

<div class="p">Because they are many, and each has a root in Torah, many letters of Torah are aroused, illuminating one another, until new souls of converts are born and sinners of Israel are stirred to <span class="h">teshuvah</span>. Therefore the Sages customarily named each <span class="h">bais midrash</span> after the <span class="h">tzadik</span>/Rebbe of the students learning there: <span class="mx">"in the <span class="h">bais midrash</span> of Rebbi…"</span> On the verse <span class="v">"she went to seek Hashem"</span>: <span class="mx">"she went to the <span class="h">bais midrash</span> of Shem and Ever."</span> The <span class="h">bais midrash</span> reaches its perfection when named after true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who can arouse the root-souls through their Torah.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"How goodly are your tents, Yaakov — your dwelling places, Yisrael!"</span> — praising <span class="h">batei knesses</span> and <span class="h">batei midrash</span>. <span class="v">"As streams flowing…"</span> — they purify like a flowing stream, standing always against the soiled garments. The Torah = a spring that ever strengthens; no impurity can withstand it. The <span class="h">bais midrash</span>'s power: because there is a fixed place for Torah, in the end one is purified. A lone individual can fall; but anyone who is part of the holy <span class="h">bais midrash</span> community — not completely separating himself — will be upheld by the Torah-merit of the many until he too is purified.</div>

<!-- §18 -->
<div class="sn">§ 18 — Eight Days of <i>Chanukah</i> = Eight Threads of <i>Tzitzis</i> = Eight Priestly Garments</div>

<div class="p">Eight days of <span class="h">Chanukah</span> correspond to the eight threads of <span class="h">tzitzis</span> = white garments = wings of <span class="h">mitzvah</span> under which converts enter = the eight garments of the <span class="h">Kohein Gadol</span>, which are juxtaposed to <span class="h">Parshas</span> <span class="h">Shemen HaMenurah</span> [the oil of the <span class="h">Menorah</span>].</div>

<!-- §19 -->
<div class="sn">§ 19 — Midnight Torah Study: Awakening from Spiritual Sleep</div>

<div class="p">Rising at <span class="h">chatzos</span> [midnight] for Torah is the supreme time to arouse the root-souls. At midnight, all the souls of the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> in <span class="h">Gan Eden</span> awaken and listen together (Zohar). The <span class="h">tzadikim</span>'s souls encompass all Israel's souls. When they are aroused, all souls are aroused, illuminating one another — birthing converts and stirring sinners to <span class="h">teshuvah</span>.</div>

<div class="p">The Zohar on <span class="v">"You who dwell in gardens — companions listen to your voice"</span>: <span class="mx">"'gardens' = <span class="h">ganus</span> [disgrace] of this world. 'Companions' = the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who listen to your voice when you engage in Torah at midnight."</span> By rising from sleep and engaging in Torah, one raises one's soul and all connected souls from <span class="v">"I am asleep"</span> (the aspect of blemished souls before returning to Hashem = <span class="h">palga</span>) — raising them from the disgrace of this world. <span class="kc">Even though one is disgraced by one's deeds, the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> listen to one's voice.</span> Therefore: <span class="v">"let me hear your voice!"</span> — do not say they will despise your voice because of your disgrace; on the contrary, Hashem and the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> delight specifically in your voice. For this is His ultimate <span class="h">kavod</span>: when the most distant awaken.</div>

<div class="p">At midnight one mourns the destruction of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> (which encompasses all the <span class="h">tikkunim</span>) to arouse mercy for its rebuilding. Afterward in the morning, one dons <span class="h">tzitzis</span> (= white garments = clean covering, the opposite of soiled garments = the <span class="h">kavod</span> aspect) and <span class="h">tefillin</span> (= <span class="h">yirah</span>: <span class="v">"they shall see… and fear you"</span>). Through this one can pray. The Arizal: the essential <span class="h">tikkun</span> of <span class="h">tzitzis</span>, <span class="h">tefillin</span>, and prayer in the morning is through rising at midnight for Torah. The Sages: <span class="mx">"'at all times let your garments be white' = <span class="h">tzitzis</span>. 'And let oil upon your head never be lacking' = <span class="h">tefillin</span>."</span></div>

<!-- §20 -->
<div class="sn">§ 20 — Never Abandon Torah: Dovid's Testimony</div>

<div class="p">One must strengthen oneself enormously to engage in Torah and prayer every day, no matter what. Every time one returns to Torah, great purity is drawn. Even if afterward things go as they go — return again to Torah, until in the end one is purified. The Torah teaches the way out of all follies and evil thoughts stemming from the soiled garments: <span class="kc">it is the source of living waters that purifies from everything.</span></div>

<div class="p">Dovid said: <span class="v">"I considered my ways and returned my feet to Your testimonies."</span> In all that befalls me, I return my feet to Torah — there is no refuge but Torah. <span class="v">"I hurried and did not delay to keep Your commandments"</span> — because of the multitude of obstacles, one must act with great haste. <span class="v">"The bonds of the wicked surrounded me — Your Torah I did not forget"</span> — despite the <span class="h">kelippos</span> gathering constantly, I was stirred to engage in Torah, which purifies. <span class="v">"At midnight I rise to thank You"</span> — the essential <span class="h">tikkun</span> at midnight: Torah-study as <span class="h">Toras Chesed</span>, arousing root-souls. <span class="v">"I am a companion to all who fear You"</span> — one must connect with true <span class="h">yirei Hashem</span> attached to true <span class="h">tzadikim</span>. <span class="v">"Your kindness, Hashem, fills the earth — teach me Your statutes"</span> — may we merit <span class="h">Toras Chesed</span>.</div>

<!-- §21 -->
<div class="sn">§ 21 — "For This Let Every Pious One Pray": Wife, Torah, Death, Privy</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Berachos</span> 8a: <span class="v">"for this let every pious one pray to You at a time of finding"</span> — one sage says "this" = a wife; another = Torah; another = death; another = burial; another = the privy. The Gemara concludes: the one who said "privy" is superior to all.</div>

<div class="p">They do not disagree; all are one and interdependent. "A wife" = the holiness of the <span class="h">zivug</span>, arousing souls to draw a pure <span class="h">neshamah</span>. "Torah" = one cannot achieve this without Torah-study in the manner described. "Death and burial" = <span class="h">tikkunim</span> for Adam's sin — through death and burial, the filth decomposes in the earth; but one must pray for a good death that immediately strips the soiled garments and clothes one in clean garments (otherwise: harsh punishments, <span class="h">Gehinnom</span>, many <span class="h">gilgulim</span>). "The privy" = the essential expulsion of waste, where the good is separated from literal filth. <span class="mx">"Healer of all flesh who acts wondrously"</span> — this clarification is a great wonder. But one must merit that spiritually too the soiled garments are expelled as completely as the physical waste is expelled.</div>

<div class="p">"The privy is superior to all" = the essential prayer is that the waste be expelled, the soiled garments stripped — for from them come all obstacles, confusions, and crooked thoughts. <span class="v">"Every pious one [<span class="h">chasid</span>]"</span> — "chasid" specifically, because all this is only possible through <span class="h">chesed</span> = <span class="h">Toras Chesed</span>. One must draw close to the <span class="h">tzadik</span> who is a true <span class="h">chasid</span>, who teaches Torah for the sake of teaching — making true <span class="h">chasidim</span> in the world.</div>

<!-- §22 -->
<div class="sn">§ 22 — "Arouse the Good Inclination Against the Evil": Torah is the Only Weapon</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Berachos</span> 5a: <span class="mx">"One should always arouse the good inclination against the evil. If he overcomes it — good. If not — let him engage in Torah. If not — let him read <span class="h">Krias Shema</span>. If not — let him remember the day of death."</span></div>

<div class="p">One might ask: why not simply remember death immediately? And we see that many people remember death yet continue sinning! The answer: <span class="kc">the only real weapon against the <span class="h">yetzer hara</span> is Torah.</span> The Sages called it <span class="h">m'nuvval</span> [repulsive] — for its power comes from the soiled garments' repulsiveness. The remedy: Torah's waters. <span class="kc">As I heard from Rabbainu's holy mouth:</span> he said he greatly desired to bring the world to action — that every person should be obligated to learn a fixed amount daily according to his level. I asked him: "But there are people who are <span class="h">ba'alei aveiros</span>!" He answered with a slight rebuke: <span class="mx">"You do not know the great power of Torah!"</span> His intention: even one trapped in sins, if he would establish fixed Torah-learning every single day, the Torah's power would eventually overcome — and he would be rectified and emerge from them in the end.</div>

<div class="p">The <span class="h">Baal Davar</span>'s primary strategy is to push a person away from Torah entirely — arguing: "You've been learning and you're still the same!" But one who honestly remembers the day of death — that his days will pass like a fleeting shadow and he must give an accounting — will not listen to this deception. He will maintain fixed Torah-study no matter what. And then: <span class="mx">"the illumination within it will return him to good."</span> Remembering death is not itself the weapon — it is what drives one <span class="kc">back to Torah</span>, which is the actual weapon. The teaching is a circle: its end is embedded in its beginning.</div>

<!-- §23 -->
<div class="sn">§ 23 — Direct Your Mind to the <i>Tzadik</i></div>

<div class="p">Every person must direct his mind — especially when engaging in Torah and <span class="h">mitzvos</span> — to draw upon himself the illumination from the true <span class="h">tzadik</span> who arouses root-souls through Torah. Though the <span class="h">tzadik</span> labors to bring illumination even to sinners of Israel, each person must also arouse himself from below: <span class="v">"do not arouse the love until it desires."</span> The more one directs one's mind and engages in Torah, the more illumination flows — <span class="v">"I am for my Beloved, and my Beloved is for me."</span></div>

<div class="p">This is the <span class="h">mitzvah</span> of lighting <span class="h">ner Chanukah</span> in every generation: through the holy act of lighting, one draws upon oneself the illumination from the root of one's <span class="h">neshamah</span> in Torah, which shines within the collective of worthy Israel through the true <span class="h">tzadik</span>'s Torah-study. Each year when Israel fulfills this <span class="h">mitzvah</span>, the illumination is drawn anew. Each time the forces of <span class="h">Malchus Yavan</span> [Greece] = <span class="h">y'ven metzulah</span> [miry depths] attempt to overpower — seeking <span class="mx">"to make them forget Your Torah and transgress Your statutes"</span> — the great <span class="h">tzadikim</span> of that time overcome them through the holiness of their Torah. Not only did they overcome them then — they established the miracle for all generations, commanding us to light <span class="h">ner Chanukah</span> each year. Even amidst whatever one endures, one can light the holy <span class="h">ner Chanukah</span> in one's home — drawing the Torah's illumination from its root through the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> of every generation.</div>

<!-- §24 -->
<div class="sn">§ 24 — <i>V'Atah Tetzaveh</i>: Moshe, the <i>Menorah</i>, and Every Soul</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"And you shall command the children of Israel, and they shall bring to you pure olive oil…"</span> — <span class="mx">"to you"</span> specifically. Moshe = the totality of Torah = the true <span class="h">tzadik</span> who arouses the root-souls. From him, the seven branches of the <span class="h">Menorah</span> — the collective souls of Israel in seven categories — are kindled.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"You shall command [<span class="h">tetzaveh</span>]"</span> — from the root <span class="h">tzavsa</span> [companionship/joining]: <span class="mx">"you shall join and unite the children of Israel"</span> — their root-souls in Torah, so the illumination reaches each one. And upon Israel it says: <span class="v">"they shall bring to you pure olive oil"</span> — each person must bring to the <span class="h">tzadik</span> the "pure olive oil" = the good point in his <span class="h">da'as</span> and mind. Even in the worst sinners, some good point remains = pure olive oil. One must prepare oneself to bring it to the <span class="h">tzadik</span> by forcing oneself to engage in Torah, prayer, and <span class="h">mitzvos</span> — with the sole intention that the <span class="h">tzadik</span>'s light should take hold in one's <span class="h">neshamah</span>, kindling it like a flame: <span class="v">"the lamp of Hashem is the soul of man."</span></div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Crushed for illumination"</span> — <span class="mx">"crushed"</span> specifically: one must crush and break the body, enduring many sufferings and bitternesses. <span class="mx">"A tree in which light does not catch — beat it. A body in which the soul's light does not catch — beat it"</span> (Zohar). The Midrash: <span class="mx">"Why are Israel compared to an olive? Just as the olive — while on the tree it is harvested, then beaten, then brought to the press, then ground, then bound with ropes, then pressed with stones — and only then does its oil flow. So too Israel…"</span> — one must endure many labors to strip the soiled garments so the <span class="h">tzadik</span>'s illumination can take hold. <span class="v">"To kindle a perpetual light"</span> — <span class="mx">"perpetual"</span>: one must force oneself to engage in Torah always, every day: <span class="v">"you shall meditate in it day and night."</span></div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"In the Tent of Meeting, outside the Curtain before the Testimony, he shall arrange it."</span> The <span class="h">Menorah</span>'s lighting faces the <span class="h">Aron HaEdus</span> [Ark of Testimony] containing the <span class="h">Luchos</span> = the root of Torah = the root of all Israel's souls: <span class="v">"its interior lined with love."</span> The <span class="h">Menorah</span>'s purpose = to draw upon Israel the illumination from their root-souls in Torah through the <span class="h">tzadik</span>. This is the essence of <span class="h">ner Chanukah</span>. The great miracle: in the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>, only the <span class="h">Kohein</span> could kindle the <span class="h">Menorah</span> in the <span class="h">Haichal</span>. Now — through the miracle when the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span> tried to abolish the Temple service entirely — the opposite occurred: <span class="kc">every Jew can kindle <span class="h">ner Chanukah</span> in his own home</span>, drawing the holiness of the <span class="h">Menorah</span>'s light upon himself.</div>

<!-- §25 -->
<div class="sn">§ 25 — <i>B'Ha'aloscha</i>: Directing All Souls Toward the <i>Tzadik</i></div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"When you kindle the lamps, toward the face of the <span class="h">Menorah</span> shall the seven lamps cast their light."</span> Aharon the <span class="h">Kohein</span> = <span class="h">shushvina d'Matrunisa</span> [the <span class="h">Shechinah</span>'s attendant], who labors to draw Israel close: <span class="mx">"be among the students of Aharon — loving peace… and bringing them close to Torah."</span></div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"When you kindle the lamps"</span> = when you wish to illuminate the seven lamps (= all Israel's soul-categories), <span class="v">"toward the face of the <span class="h">Menorah</span>"</span> = toward the true <span class="h">tzadik</span> = Moshe = the face of the <span class="h">Menorah</span> / the face of Torah — for he encompasses all seventy facets of Torah and can therefore illuminate all root-souls. All categories of Israel must direct their minds toward the illumination of the true <span class="h">tzadik</span>, from whom their souls shine.</div>

<!-- §26 -->
<div class="sn">§ 26 — The <i>Haftarah</i> of <i>Chanukah</i>: <i>Menorah</i> and Soiled Garments</div>

<div class="p">In Zechariah (chapter 4) — the <span class="h">Haftarah</span> of <span class="h">Shabbos Chanukah</span> — the vision of the <span class="h">Menorah</span> is juxtaposed to the passage about Yehoshua who <span class="v">"was clothed in soiled garments,"</span> and the command: <span class="v">"remove the soiled garments!"</span> For the <span class="h">Menorah</span> = the <span class="h">tikkun</span> and purification that removes soiled garments and brings white garments: <span class="v">"at all times let your garments be white, and oil upon your head never be lacking."</span></div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Like the good oil… descending upon his garments"</span> — the oil (= Torah-illumination) reaches the garments themselves, transforming them into clean priestly garments.</div>

<div class="ts">
  <div class="sh">✦ Translator's Summary — Part 2 (§§ 14–26) ✦</div>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>§§14-15 — Chanukah oil</strong>: Oil = the holy <em>da'as</em> of Torah flowing through the true <em>tzadikim</em>. "Oil upon your head never be lacking" = Torah must never cease. Through this, garments become white. <em>Mosif v'holech</em> = adding light daily until the eighth day (= <em>Binah</em>, root of Torah). Eight days = eight threads of <em>tzitzis</em> = eight priestly garments.</p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>§§16-17 — <em>Bais HaMikdash</em> and <em>batei midrash</em></strong>: The Temple encompasses all <em>tikkunim</em>: Torah, purification, <em>kavod</em>, converts, <em>yirah</em>, <em>shalom</em>. Yerushalayim = <em>yirah</em> + <em>shalom</em>. <em>Batei midrash</em> = <em>mikdash me'at</em>. Named after the <em>tzadik</em> whose Torah animates them. The communal merit protects even the individual who would otherwise fall.</p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>§§19-20 — Midnight study</strong>: At <em>chatzos</em>, the <em>tzadikim</em> in <em>Gan Eden</em> awaken and listen. Even one disgraced by his deeds — the <em>tzadikim</em> delight in his Torah voice. Mourning the Temple at midnight → <em>tzitzis</em> and <em>tefillin</em> in the morning. Dovid's testimony: always return to Torah; Torah is the only refuge.</p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>§21 — Wife, Torah, death, privy</strong>: All one teaching. The privy is "superior to all" because expelling waste = the ultimate clarification. All through <em>Toras Chesed</em>.</p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>§22 — Rabbainu's words</strong>: <em>"You do not know the great power of Torah!"</em> Even one trapped in sins — fixed daily Torah-learning will eventually rectify him. Remember death → return to Torah → Torah overcomes all.</p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>§§23-26 — <em>Ner Chanukah</em> and the <em>Menorah</em></strong>: Direct your mind to the <em>tzadik</em> → <em>Ani l'Dodi v'Dodi li</em>. "Pure olive oil" = the good point in every soul. "Crushed for illumination" = the body must be broken through suffering. Every Jew can kindle <em>ner Chanukah</em> at home = drawing the Temple's <em>Menorah</em>-light. Zechariah's <em>Haftarah</em>: <em>Menorah</em> juxtaposed to soiled garments being removed.</p>
</div>

